      "dom_id": "question-65052",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate ${f}_{yyx}(1,0,1)$ for $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xyz}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xyz}\\cdot xz \\\\  & =xz{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{yy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((xz{e}^{xyz})) \\\\  & =xz{e}^{xyz}\\cdot xz \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{z}^{2}{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{yyx}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{yy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yyx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & ={z}^{2}[2x\\cdot {e}^{xyz}+{x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}\\cdot yz] \\\\  & =x{z}^{2}(2+xyz){e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{yyx}(x,y,z)$ at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yyx}(1,0,1) & =1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot (2+1\\cdot 0\\cdot 1)\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0\\cdot 1}=1\\cdot (2+0)\\cdot {e}^{0}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
