      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate ${f}_{yyx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}_{yyx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yyx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\cdot y \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
